  "articleBody": "So, you've just installed your brand-new handheld showerhead, and a drip, drip, drip has started. Annoying, right? There are a few usual culprits when it comes to leaks after a handheld showerhead installation. First, let's talk about overtightening. It's a super common mistake! You might think tighter is better, but cranking down on fittings with a wrench when they're only designed for hand-tightening can actually damage the threads or gaskets, creating a leak point. Another big one is not using the correct seal. Some handheld showerheads require thread seal tape on the shower arm, while others rely on specific rubber washers, or even both! If you've missed a washer or didn't apply the tape correctly, water will find a way out. Also, make sure you've cleaned off any old tape or residue from the existing shower arm threads before putting on the new setup. Even tiny bits can create an uneven surface and cause a leak. Lastly, double-check that the hose ends and any vacuum breaker parts are installed in the right orientation. If you've tried these quick fixes and your showerhead is still leaking, especially if it's a persistent drip or you're dealing with low flow, it’s probably time to call in a professional like HH Remodeling LLC. We can quickly diagnose the problem and ensure your showerhead is installed correctly, without any sneaky leaks.",
  "datePublished": "2026-08-09"
